                <pb facs="0359" n="289" sameAs="frus:frus1934v04/pg289"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/3761: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Bolivia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Des Portes</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-05-29T17:00:00-04:00">May 29, 1934—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>34. Referring to Department’s circular May 28, 9 p.m. The information was today
                    given the Foreign Minister who commented <pb facs="0360" n="290" xml:id="pg290"
                    /> his opinion that the Resolution is contrary to article VI Treaty of
                        1858<anchor n="16" xml:id="d360-f1"/> and Hughes Convention at
                        Habana;<anchor n="17" xml:id="d360-f2"/> that the Resolution unfair since
                    Bolivia needs material for defense particularly regarding plans invasion Santa
                    Cruz and geographic inequality the two countries for receiving supplies; he also
                    wishes to know if Resolution retroactive.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Des Portes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
